<div class="container"><div class="lesson-body">
<h2>Seiberg-Witten Theory — Monopoles, Gauge Theory &amp; 4-Manifold Invariants</h2>
<p>Deep dive into Seiberg-Witten theory — the Seiberg–Witten equations (a system of partial differential equations coupling a U(1) connection A to a spinor field ψ: the Dirac equation D̸_A ψ = 0 and the curvature equation F_A⁺ = (ψ⊗ψ*)_₀, where F_A⁺ is the self-dual part of the curvature and (ψ⊗ψ*)_₀ is the trace-free part of the bilinear form), the Seiberg–Witten invariants (a diffeomorphism invariant of smooth 4-manifolds defined using the moduli space of solutions modulo gauge equivalence, which is a compact smooth manifold of dimension d = (c₁(L)² − (2χ + 3σ))/4 for a Spinc-structure with determinant line bundle L, the Seiberg–Witten invariant SW(L) is the count of points in the moduli space when d = 0, otherwise the evaluation of the top cohomology class of the moduli space), the wall-crossing formula (the change of the Seiberg–Witten invariant under a change of metric or perturbation, given by a formula involving the harmonic 2-forms), the relationship with Donaldson theory (the Witten conjecture — Seiberg–Witten invariants are equivalent to Donaldson invariants, proved by Feehan and Leness for most 4-manifolds using the PU(2) monopole equations), and the applications to the geography problem for 4-manifolds (the Noether inequality, the Bogomolov–Miyaoka–Yau inequality, the classification of symplectic 4-manifolds via Taubes's theorem: a symplectic 4-manifold has a non-vanishing Seiberg–Witten invariant).</p>
<div class="quote-box"><div class="quote">"Seiberg–Witten theory revolutionized the study of 4-manifolds by providing a simpler yet equally powerful alternative to Donaldson theory."</div><div class="attribution">— Edward Witten</div></div>
<div class="section-label part1">Part 1</div>
<h2>🧲 Monopoles and the Seiberg-Witten Equations</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Monopoles</div><div class="story-title">The Seiberg–Witten Equations: Dirac Operator and Curvature</div><div class="story-text">Let M be a closed oriented 4-manifold with a Spinc-structure (a lift of the structure group of TM from SO(4) to Spinc(4) = (Spin(4) × U(1))/ℤ₂). A Spinc-structure determines a <strong>determinant line bundle</strong> L = det⁺(W⁺) (the line bundle associated to the U(1) factor) and a pair of spinor bundles W⁺, W⁻ (complex rank 2 and 1 respectively). The <strong>Seiberg–Witten equations</strong> for a pair (A, ψ) where A is a unitary connection on L and ψ is a section of W⁺ are:
(1) The <strong>Dirac equation</strong>: D̸_A ψ = 0, where D̸_A: Γ(W⁺) → Γ(W⁻) is the Dirac operator coupled to A.
(2) The <strong>curvature equation</strong>: F_A⁺ = (ψ⊗ψ*)_₀, where F_A⁺ = (F_A + *F_A)/2 is the self-dual part of the curvature, and (ψ⊗ψ*)_₀ = ψ⊗ψ* − (|ψ|²/2) id is the trace-free Hermitian endomorphism of W⁺.
The equations are invariant under <strong>gauge transformations</strong> g: M → U(1) acting as (A, ψ) → (A − 2 d log g, gψ). The <strong>moduli space</strong> M(L) of solutions modulo gauge equivalence is a compact oriented manifold of dimension d(L) = c₁(L)² − (2χ(M) + 3σ(M))/4 (where χ(M) is the Euler characteristic and σ(M) is the signature). The <strong>perturbed Seiberg–Witten equations</strong> F_A⁺ = (ψ⊗ψ*)_₀ + iη (where η is a self-dual 2-form) are used to achieve transversality — for a generic perturbation η, the moduli space is smooth.</div><div class="story-moral">The Seiberg–Witten equations couple a gauge field to a spinor; solutions (monopoles) form a compact moduli space whose dimension is computed by characteristic classes of the Spinc-structure.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part2">Part 2</div>
<h2>🏆 Seiberg-Witten Invariants of 4-Manifolds</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Invariants</div><div class="story-title">Definition of Seiberg–Witten Invariants, Wall-Crossing, and Vanishing Theorems</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>Seiberg–Witten invariant</strong> SW_M(L) ∈ ℤ for a Spinc-structure with determinant L is defined as follows. When the moduli space M(L) has formal dimension d = 0, the invariant is the signed count of points in M(L) (with sign determined by orientations of the determinant line bundle of the Dirac operator). When d > 0, SW_M(L) is the evaluation of the top cohomology class (the <strong>Euler class</strong> of a certain line bundle over M(L) raised to the power d/2) against the fundamental class. The <strong>wall-crossing formula</strong> (Seiberg–Witten, 1994): when the dimension d(L) = 0, the Seiberg–Witten invariant changes by ±1 under a change of metric that crosses a wall where the moduli space becomes reducible (i.e., ψ = 0 solutions exist). The walls correspond to <strong>harmonic self-dual 2-forms</strong> η satisfying ∫_M η ∧ c₁(L) = 0. The <strong>vanishing theorem</strong> (Witten): if a closed 4-manifold M has b₂⁺(M) ≥ 1 and admits a metric of positive scalar curvature, then all Seiberg–Witten invariants vanish. Conversely, if SW_M(L) ≠ 0 for some L, then M admits no metric of positive scalar curvature. The <strong>Kronheimer–Mrowka theorem</strong>: if M is a 4-manifold with b₂⁺(M) ≥ 2, the Seiberg–Witten invariants are diffeomorphism invariants (independent of the choice of metric and perturbation). The <strong>simple type conjecture</strong> (Taubes, Kronheimer–Mrowka): for many 4-manifolds, all Seiberg–Witten invariants vanish unless d(L) = 0 — the <strong>simple type condition</strong>.</div><div class="story-moral">Seiberg–Witten invariants are powerful diffeomorphism invariants of smooth 4-manifolds; they detect the existence of positive scalar curvature metrics and distinguish smooth structures.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part3">Part 3</div>
<h2>🔗 Relationship with Donaldson Theory</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Donaldson</div><div class="story-title">The Witten Conjecture: Equivalence of Seiberg–Witten and Donaldson Invariants</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Donaldson theory</strong> (Donaldson, 1982) defined invariants of smooth 4-manifolds using the moduli space of anti-self-dual (ASD) connections on a principal SU(2)-bundle. The <strong>Donaldson polynomial invariants</strong> q_m: H₂(M) → ℤ are determined by the cohomology ring of the ASD moduli space. The <strong>Witten conjecture</strong> (1994): the Donaldson invariants are equivalent to the Seiberg–Witten invariants via the formula:
D_{M,w}(z) = Σ_{L} SW_M(L) exp(⟨c₁(L), z⟩/2)
where the sum is over all Spinc-structures L whose first Chern class is characteristic (c₁(L) ≡ w₂(M) mod 2). This was motivated by the <strong>physical equivalence</strong> of N = 2 supersymmetric Yang–Mills theory and the Abelian monopole theory via <strong>Seiberg–Witten duality</strong> (Seiberg–Witten, 1994): the low-energy effective action of N = 2 SU(2) SYM is described by a <strong>Seiberg–Witten curve</strong> and a <strong>prepotential</strong> that encodes the Donaldson invariants. The proof of the Witten conjecture was completed by <strong>Feehan–Leness</strong> (2003) using the <strong>PU(2) monopole equations</strong> — a non-abelian generalization of the Seiberg–Witten equations that interpolates between the ASD equations and the abelian Seiberg–Witten equations via a <strong>blow-up</strong> analysis. The <strong>Kotschick–Morgan–Taubes</strong> theorem gives an alternative proof for Kähler surfaces. The equivalence shows that Seiberg–Witten theory is strictly easier to work with computationally while capturing the same information: the <strong>Seiberg–Witten invariants</strong> determine the Donaldson invariants, and vice versa.</div><div class="story-moral">The Witten conjecture (proved by Feehan–Leness) establishes the equivalence of the two great gauge-theoretic invariants of 4-manifolds, showing Seiberg–Witten theory subsumes Donaldson theory.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part4">Part 4</div>
<h2>🌊 Applications to Symplectic Geometry and Contact Topology</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Applications</div><div class="story-title">Taubes's Theorem, Symplectic 4-Manifolds, and Contact Invariants</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Taubes's theorem</strong> (1994–1996): Let (M, ω) be a closed symplectic 4-manifold with b₂⁺ ≥ 1. Then for the <strong>canonical Spinc-structure</strong> K (whose determinant line bundle is the canonical bundle K = ∧²⁺T*M), the Seiberg–Witten invariant satisfies SW_M(K) = ±1. For all other Spinc-structures, the Seiberg–Witten invariant is zero. This remarkable result implies that the Seiberg–Witten invariants detect the symplectic structure: a 4-manifold with non-vanishing Seiberg–Witten invariants admits a symplectic structure if and only if the basic classes are related by the adjunction formula. The <strong>adjunction inequality</strong>: for any smoothly embedded surface Σ ⊂ M of genus g(Σ), we have 2g(Σ) − 2 ≥ ⟨c₁(L), Σ⟩ + Σ · Σ whenever SW_M(L) ≠ 0 — providing powerful restrictions on embeddings of surfaces. The <strong>symplectic Thom conjecture</strong> (proved by Ozsváth–Szabó and Taubes): a symplectic surface in a symplectic 4-manifold minimizes the genus in its homology class. The <strong>contact Seiberg–Witten invariants</strong> (Kronheimer–Mrowka, 1995): for a contact 3-manifold (Y, ξ), there is a <strong>contact invariant</strong> in the monopole Floer homology of Y that detects the overtwisted vs. tight classification. The <strong>Weinstein conjecture</strong> — that every contact structure on a closed 3-manifold has a closed Reeb orbit — was proved by <strong>Taubes</strong> (2007) using Seiberg–Witten theory. The <strong>embedded contact homology (ECH)</strong> (Hutchings, 2002) is a topological invariant of 3-manifolds defined using Seiberg–Witten theory and holomorphic curves, and Taubes proved ECH ≅ SWF (Seiberg–Witten Floer homology).</div><div class="story-moral">Seiberg–Witten theory has profound applications to symplectic and contact geometry: it detects symplectic structures, constrains surface embeddings, and computes contact invariants.</div></div>
